+# Case-Insensitive Specialty Matching Lives in the Query, Not the Database Collation
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+REQ-VET-001 and REQ-VET-003 narrow the veterinarian directory to one specialty. The match is on the whole name and disregards letter case, on every supported database.
+
+Owner search already relies on vendor collation for the same guarantee, and it does not hold. H2 types the owner last name `VARCHAR_IGNORECASE`, MySQL defaults to a case-insensitive collation, and PostgreSQL compares case-sensitively. The same search therefore returns different results per vendor — the defect recorded against `REQ-OWN-002`.
+
+The `specialties.name` column repeats the setup exactly: `VARCHAR(80)` on H2, `TEXT` on PostgreSQL, and a default-collated `VARCHAR(80)` on MySQL. A match written as a plain equality would be case-insensitive on MySQL alone.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Rely on vendor collation** — plain equality on the name, as owner search does.
+2. **Case-fold in the query** — the repository asks for a case-insensitive comparison, and the JPA provider emits `upper(name) = upper(?)` on every vendor.
+3. **Store a normalized name** — add a lower-cased column or a functional index per vendor, and match on it.
+
+## Decision
+
+We case-fold in the query. The derived Spring Data finder carries the `IgnoreCase` keyword, so the provider generates the same case-folding comparison for all three vendors.
+
+Option 1 was rejected because it reproduces a known defect in new code. Option 3 was rejected because it edits three hand-maintained schemas to buy an index the seeded specialty list is far too small to need.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The narrowing behaves identically on H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, and an H2-backed test proves the behavior the other vendors get.
+- The index on `specialties.name` is not used by the case-folded comparison. The specialty list holds three rows, so the scan cost is nil.
+- The case-folding comparison is the pattern available when the owner-search defect is repaired. That repair is not made here.
+- Case-folding is applied by the database, so the result depends on the vendor's `upper` for non-ASCII names. The seeded specialties are ASCII.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-001, REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md — Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the repository that carries the finder.
+- [system-design.md — Known Defects](../system-design.md#known-defects) — the owner-search divergence this decision avoids repeating.
+- [Narrowing the Veterinarian Directory Is In Scope](2026-08-25-non-goal-vet-directory-filtering.md) — the scope decision this one serves.
